And it will screw up warm-up, no? Optimizing sampler params when one or several parameters has nothing to optimize against (it's disconnected from the shape of posterior, so the simulated particle can go in any direction without any change in the hamiltonian). The warm-up would adapt to a parameter that shouldn't be adapted to, but that can screw up the sampling of all other parameters. p(unused | D, other params) \sim constant; warmup would adapt to something that makes no sense and cause divergences during sampling, no?

On Thursday, January 5, 2017 at 11:58:36 AM UTC-6, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Thursday, January 5, 2017 at 12:42:46 PM UTC-5, Pedro Carvalho wrote:
> > These parametres are all strictly positive; is there an equivalent thing to the unit-scale for those? And why do extra parametres affect convergence for parametres they're not connected to?
>
> Because it it is trying to draw from a joint posterior distribution and any parameters that are unused and do not have proper priors make the joint posterior distribution improper.
>

