> To go into more detail, I been using STAN to try and do some cosmology fitting for my PhD. It's going well, but I am still very new to STAN and to Bayesian methods in general. There is one part of the model which is too complicated to be implemented in STAN (have to do MC integration instead)

^^^
Sorry, my guess is that it's not. At least, I'd try hard to not approximate first. Or start with something simpler if this is your first time really using Stan. What exactly is too complicated for Stan? (Trust me, there are things, but I've seen enough people assume they need to use an approximation when it is better just doing the right thing.)

Without a description of what you're doung, preferably in program form (programs don't lie), it would be hard to help you with the approximation or the actual computation.

> , and so I have implemented an approximation in STAN, and then reweight the STAN chains afterwards to adjust for the difference between the approximate correction and the actual correction (the approximate weight (ie efficiency) is recorded by stan, so I can reweight after the fact by weighting each step by approximate_efficiency - actual_efficiency) . Now here is where it gets interesting - when I change the approximation my posterior surfaces shift - and this should not be happening because mathematically the approximation cancels out, because its contribution in STAN is removed in the reweighting. In my bid to understand what is going on, I think I first need to understand why the samples are distributed as they are in logposterior space.
