From u5d67cef991a09367 at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 18 01:00:30 2017
From: u5d67cef991a09367 at groups.google.com (Tadaishi Yatabe)
Date: Wed, 18 Jan 2017 01:00:30 +0000
Subject: Truncated generalized poisson
Message-ID: <RJS74VArneg.EeOeaOVxAQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/RJS74VArneg/m/EeOeaOVxAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello dear list,

I've been trying, with big help from you, to fit a generalized Poisson model for underdispersed count data, which I finally managed to do. The main ideas, which are well pointed out by Scollnik in this paper http://www.actuaries.org/LIBRARY/ASTIN/vol28no1/135.pdf , are namely: when the are negatives values of lambda the pmf of the distribution is no longer normalized (does not sum to one), so this distribution should be normalized to solve this issue. The normalization is based on the sumation of probabilities of observing counts ranging from 0 to m, where m is the upper bound in the number of "observable" counts. m could be fixed to be given known number, M, or could also be distribution, such as m~uniform(1, M), or even m ~ poisson(mu).
Now my questions are
1) How to normalize the probabilities?
2) How to use a distribution of the parameter m in this model. I understand that in Stan it's not possible to use distribution of integers in a model

Finally, in a perhaps different subject, how to obtain WAIC once a model is fit, to do model comparison. I tried to get the likelihoods with the package RMK discrete, but it generated lots of NAs, I suppose this is related with the fact that my distribution is not normalized(?) Anyway, perhaps there's a better way to do this.

I'd appreciate any help with this subject.

Thanks,

Tada

Below is the code and data I've used to run a model in which the distribution is not truncated:

functions{
real lb(matrix X, vector beta){
real max_mu = max(exp(X * beta));
if (max_mu <= 2) return max_mu / (max_mu - 4);
return -1;
  }
}

data {
int<lower=0> N;
int<lower=0> K;
matrix[N, K] X;
vector<lower=0>[N] y;
}
transformed data {
vector[N] ym1 = y - 1;
}

parameters {
vector[K] beta;
real<lower=lb(X, beta), upper = 1> lambda;
}

model {

vector[N] eta = X * beta;
vector[N] theta = exp(eta) * (1 - lambda);
vector[N] theta_lambda_y = theta + lambda * y;
vector[N] logyfact =  lgamma(y + 1);

target += (eta + log1m(lambda) + ym1 .* log(theta_lambda_y) - theta_lambda_y - logyfact);

lambda ~ normal(0,0.1);
beta[1] ~ normal(0,10);
beta[2:4] ~ normal(0,1);

}

From u71c315ee557ff234 at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 18 02:01:04 2017
From: u71c315ee557ff234 at groups.google.com (andre.p...@googlemail.com)
Date: Wed, 18 Jan 2017 02:01:04 +0000
Subject: Re: Truncated generalized poisson
Message-ID: <RJS74VArneg.p2EcajN1AQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <RJS74VArneg.EeOeaOVxAQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <RJS74VArneg.EeOeaOVxAQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/RJS74VArneg/m/p2EcajN1AQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Tada,

1.
dgenpois(0:100,-0.3, .1) / sum(dgenpois(0:100,-0.3, .1),na.rm =T)
taking sum and dividing does the normalization.

2. You should pick m beforehand. m is usually small, so you can
adjust prior and see what best suits.

My 2 cents: Just pick m, that your genpoisson behaves smoothly.

There is a package 'loo' does the WAIC for you, just write
 log_lik[i] = (eta + log1m(lambda) + ym1 .* log(theta_lambda_y) - theta_lambda_y - logyfact);

in generated quantities and later on you do waic with extract_log_lik function.

Andre

From ud8b934109b5119b2 at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 18 10:09:23 2017
From: ud8b934109b5119b2 at groups.google.com (Daniel Padfield)
Date: Wed, 18 Jan 2017 10:09:23 +0000
Subject: Hard coding a dataset and parameter relationship in Stan which works in Jags
Message-ID: <XvixdZIeHqw.pCa6HtmPAQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/XvixdZIeHqw/m/pCa6HtmPAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Folks

I am just starting out on my Bayesian adventures and am just trying to get my head round some code my supervisor has given me from another collaborator.

We have a bunch of community metabolic rate data that is dependent on temperature and mass. We have 1 temperature point per rate point but variable numbers of mass points (as there are > 1 individuals that make each community).

The relationship of metabolic rate with mass is a power law at the individual level and my collaborator hard-coded it into the jags model from the raw mass points. A variable for each rate variable is created by log(mass_1^a + .... mass_i^a). A simplified example for 3 points of rate and temperature would be:

'model{
  # priors
  bT ~ dnorm(0, 0.001)        # effect of temperature
  a ~ dnorm(0, 0.001)         # size scaling exponent
  ln_r0 ~ dnorm(0, 0.001)     # community normalisation constant
  tau_R ~ dgamma(1.0E-3,1.0E-3)
  var_R <- 1/tau_R

  # size corrected biomass
  ln.biomass[1] <- log(
  pow(2.54, a) + pow(3.28, a) + pow(3.54, a) + pow(2.64, a))
  ln.biomass[2] <- log(
  pow(2.64, a) + pow(2.61, a) + pow(2.67, a) + pow(3.36, a) + pow(2.86, a))
  ln.biomass[3] <- log(
  pow(2.65, a) + pow(3.60, a) + pow(3.39, a))

  for(i in 1:length(ln_rate)){
    ln_rate[i] ~ dnorm(mu[i], tau_R)
    mu[i] <- log(exp(ln_r0) * exp(ln.biomass[i])) + bT*temp[i]
  }
}
'

I was wondering how I could go about hard coding this relationship in Stan or whether there was another way of doing it in Stan using arrays or matrices.

So far I have tried to alter the jags code to Stan by putting the ln.biomass[] code into the model{} specification but I somehow doubt this is the best way to do such things.

'data{
  int N;                              // number of samples
  vector[N] ln_rate;                  // rate
  vector[N] temp;                     // temperature
}
parameters{
  real<lower=0> sigma;      // standard deviation
  real ln_r0;               // community normalisation constant
  real bT;                  // effect of temperature
  real a;                   // size scaling exponent
}
model{
  vector[N] mu;
  vector[N] ln_biomass;
  ln_r0 ~ normal(0,10);
  bT ~ normal(0,10);
  a ~ normal(0,10);
  sigma ~ cauchy(0,2);

  ln_biomass[1] = log(
    pow(2.54, a) + pow(3.28, a) + pow(3.54, a) + pow(2.64, a));
  ln_biomass[2] = log(
    pow(2.64, a) + pow(2.61, a) + pow(2.67, a) + pow(3.36, a) + pow(2.86, a));
  ln_biomass[3] = log(
    pow(2.65, a) + pow(3.60, a) + pow(3.39, a));

  # likelihood regression for size dependence of rate
  for(i in 1:N) {
    mu[i]  = log(exp(ln_r0) * exp(ln_biomass[i])) + bT*temp;
  }
  ln_rate ~  normal(mu, sigma);
}'

Any advice on how best to code this would be much appreciated.

Many thanks
Dan

From u3f37be0cf708d442 at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 18 11:06:33 2017
From: u3f37be0cf708d442 at groups.google.com (Guido Biele)
Date: Wed, 18 Jan 2017 11:06:33 +0000
Subject: Problem estimating covariance matrix in imputation model
Message-ID: <7DRFGn36k-o.GTpJwveSAQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/7DRFGn36k-o/m/GTpJwveSAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

The model below imputes missing data from a multivariate

normal distribution (for some variable after applying a logit transform).

This works well in most cases, but for some data some chains do not manage

to estimates the covariance matrix and means of the mvn.

At first I thought the problem might be that I have highly correlated data that result

in a non-invertible covariance martrix. While I indeed begin to observe

problems when the determinant of the cov matrix of the complete data drops from

0.14 to 0.07, the determinant never becomes smaller than 0.006 and some chains always

manage to estimate the covariance matrix and means of the mvn.

So stan definitively can estimate those parameters, just not consistently

with each chain with the model below.

It would be nice if anybody could look at the model code below and see if

I am doing something wrong.

Best - Guido

data {
  int<lower = 1> N;      # total N
  int<lower = 1> D;      # number DVs
  matrix[N,D] y;         # outcome seperately for DVs
  int<lower=0, upper=1> logit_transf[D]; # 1 if logit transform is to be applied
  int missings[N,D];
  int nmiss[D];
}

transformed data{
  matrix[N,D] y_normal;
  int missing_counter[N,D];
  int missingL[N,D];
  int imp_idx_start[D];
  int imp_idx_end[D];
  {
    int mc;
    mc = 0;
    for(v in 1:D) for (n in 1:N) {
      missingL[n,v] =  missings[n,v] > 0 ? 1 : 0;
      mc = missingL[n,v] ? mc+1 : mc;
      missing_counter[n,v] =  missingL[n,v] ? mc : 0;
      y_normal[n,v] = missingL[n,v] ? 0 : (logit_transf[v] ? logit(y[n,v]) : y[n,v]);
    }
  }
}

parameters {
  cholesky_factor_corr[D] i_L_Omega; // correlation matrix
  vector[D] i_mu;
  real imps[sum(nmiss)];
}

model {
  row_vector[D] y_normal_i[N];
  //matrix[N,D] y_i;

  i_mu ~ normal(0,3);
  i_L_Omega ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(2);
  imps ~ normal(0,2);

  for (v in 1:D) {
    // no missing data
    if (nmiss[v] == 0) {
      for (n in 1:N) {
        y_normal_i[n,v] = y_normal[n,v];
      }
    // missings data
    } else {
      // imputation on unbounded (normally distributed) scale
      for (n in 1:N) {
        y_normal_i[n,v] = missingL[n,v] ? imps[missing_counter[n,v]] : y_normal[n,v];
      }
    }
  }
  // log posterior for imputations
  y_normal_i ~ multi_normal_cholesky(i_mu, i_L_Omega);
}

From uee3aa4887fb0b91c at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 18 13:42:46 2017
From: uee3aa4887fb0b91c at groups.google.com (Pierre Lebrun)
Date: Wed, 18 Jan 2017 13:42:46 +0000
Subject: Coverage - beta expectation
Message-ID: <w-IE2-yDMDo.xwf2Cn6bAQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/w-IE2-yDMDo/m/xwf2Cn6bAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

Best wishes first to your incredible team !

Some months ago, I came up with having different results <https://groups.google.com/d/msg/stan-users/5Y5_HWZ0SCY/46toOmSJCgAJ> when using different parametrizations of the same model (mainly, in term of sd and in term of precision = 1/sd^2). I realised at this time that intervals obtained with the "sd" parametrization were extremely large when degrees of freedom (df) were low, for both the parameters and the predictions (beta-expectation tolerance intervals). When I said "large" at this time, I already meant "larger than what is expected because of low df".

At the time, we had some discussion on priors, on Jacobians, on some additional good advice for model implementation, etc., and the last point not yet discussed was to check carefully the coverage; namely, to see if the obtained prediction intervals were of expectation beta (if beta is the expected coverage).

So, I did the simulations. Here, with a simple linear regression (assume it is a model for a variable recorded along the time). I'll look at the coverage for the prediction of a single time point. I fixed beta at 95% (because as a statistician, I cannot come with a better idea :-D ), and I looked at the expected/average coverage across all simulations.

Four files are attached, all self running (data are generated each time):
- Frequentist classical prediction interval (i.e. the beta-expectation tolerance interval, in this case)
- MCMCregress (package MCMCpack) implementation of the same.
- Stan implementation of the same with uniform prior (I also try with other implementations, e.g. centred X, as done with brms package.), and inv_gamma [message edited to add it]. Might be better with inv_gamma prior for support, but uniform shouldn't lead to that much trouble.

For Bayesian framework, again, I tried to be non informative with Stan, i.e. using improper uniform prior. Indeed, in a simulation exercise, I don't want to / cannot use as prior what I know from my simulated data. Similar for inv_gamma prior. For MCMCregress, I did not specify any prior (I let the default, which is similar to one would assumed non-informative with conjugate prior, i.e. inverse_gamma(0.0001,0.0001) for sigma^2).

Results are all good for both the Frequentist estimates and Bayesian MCMCregress results (on plots, blue line must be on top of the red line, located at beta=95% coverage). However, Stan coverages with uniform priors are over-estimated when df are low (i.e. blue line higher than red line in small df cases, i.e. not of beta-expectation). This is really embarrassing as small data remains the common place in many domains. From previous experiments, all other Gibbs or M-H based samplers perform better with low df (but really I like Stan language and flexibilities so much... :-) ). Stan is "stabilized" with inv_gamma prior, but coverage are always "just a little bit too low". I would have preferred the opposite :-s.

I did not check precisely, but I am sure that the posterior distribution of the parameters does not exhibit good coverage properties in the uniform case (the predictive only borrows their own variabilities). It would mean the exploration of the posterior distribution with NUTS has some trouble here.

Fortunately, most user of samplers tend to be careful with the use of their results. But this type of issue seems more difficult to handle and detect, in my opinion (in my case, on the previous posts (see link above), intervals were so large that I did not believe in them, and I had Frequentist approximations to figure it out, but in some cases... well... it might be dangerous).
Fortunately, again, these results are conservative (intervals too large), which in most of the time protect the statistician, but not always as with the inverse gamma.

Any idea of what would cause that and if there is a way, or a plan, to improve/correct that ?

Also, keep up with the awesome work up to there.

Kind regards,
-----------
Pierre

ps: try to run the code with Stan twice in a row. On my machine, R crashes completely the second time, at the time of model compilation... That's probably a problem with parallelism, but the way it is done (Stan not in parallel, simulations in parallel) is the fastest I've found.

