MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> I am sure I will have plenty more questions but can someone confirm
> that in 1.1 of the former and 3.1 of the latter that the right hand
> side is the Radon-Nikodym derivative? Here’s the equation in LaTeX but
> I doubt googlegroups will render it :(
>
> $$
> H = -\log\frac{d(\pi^*\bar{\omega} \wedge \zeta)}{d\Omega}
> $$

Yes, it is the Radon-Nikodym derivative with respect to the canonical
symplectic measure.

> But in that case, how is the Radon-Nikodym derivative being
> calculated? Presumably in some co-ordinate system (the canonical
> co-ordinate system?), we can associate (real-valued) functions on the
> cotangent bundle, now interpreted as a measure space, with the two
> volume forms? I assume that this concept is well-defined with respect
> to changes in co-ordinates?

Recall that Radon-Nikodym derivatives are defined purely in terms of
the measures over a common sample space and need not be defined
in terms of any specific coordinate system.  The coordinate dependence
arises only when considering the base measure to be a
coordinate-dependent Lebesgue measure.

But on symplectic manifolds there is a canonical volume form/measure
that serves as a unique equivalent to the Lebesgue measure.  More
intuitively, the positions and momenta introduce inverse Jacobian
corrections upon a change of coordinates that cancel in the definition
of Omega.

