Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/B1hCluSD3R4/m/l9Dvz3Y_CwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

>
>> On Jan 26, 2017, at 11:19 AM, Luca Campanelli <campan...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>

>> ...

>> As you can see, the example below ignores that there is more than one subject. What is the best way to turn it in a multilevel model (random intercepts and slopes model)? I think the intuitive way to do it would be indexing alpha and beta for subjects, eg, alpha[subj[n]], but I’m already indexing alpha and beta for conditions. Is there any way I can use two indices so to estimate alpha and beta for each subject and condition? Or can I just add a random intercept term to the equation? Any other solution? Any difference in therm of efficiency?

Yes, you can do any of these things, but they result in different models.
The term you're looking for in the literature is "interaction".  If you
have multiple intercepts floating around, you can quickly run into non-identifiability
issues.

The best source I know for all of this is Gelman and Hill's multilevel regression
book.

- Bob

